<section class="shell band">
  <h1>Reem Glasco, LPC-S</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>Reem Glasco is a Licensed Professional Counselor Supervisor in Texas, license 66562,
    with over fifteen years of clinical experience and supervisor status since 2009. She is a
    NARM therapist, a Somatic Experiencing Practitioner (SEP), EMDR certified and an EMDRIA
    Approved Consultant. She works with young adults, college-age clients and adults, on
    developmental trauma, C-PTSD, anxiety, panic attacks, depression, and the kind of
    procrastination that does not respond to discipline. She was a Clinical Partner at Capital
    Area Counseling from 2021 to 2025, and practices from Austin, Texas: online across the
    state, and outdoors when the work asks for more than a screen.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>Most of what brings people here was learned somewhere, and learned for a reason. All
      three methods I work with start from that, and none of them asks you to push through.</p>
      <p><strong>NARM</strong> is an invitation to get curious about a pattern rather than to
      fight it. Where did I learn this? What was it doing for me then? And if I am present
      enough to notice it happening, what choice do I actually have? The answers tend to arrive
      through the felt sense rather than through analysis, which is why the work is slower than
      advice and why it holds.</p>
      <p><strong>EMDR</strong>, as I practice it, is IFS-informed: the protective parts of you
      are consulted before anything is processed, and most of my emphasis sits in the
      preparation, where that agreement gets built. It is weighted toward stabilization rather
      than excavation, which is what acute stress, ongoing stress, overwhelm and loss actually
      need.</p>
      <p><strong>Somatic Experiencing</strong> meets shock trauma a little at a time, slow and
      gentle, building your window of tolerance rather than testing it. A nervous system learns
      resilience at the pace it can hold, and not faster.</p>
      <p>I teach effortless self-care through walk and mindful therapy, outdoors, which for some
      people is the difference between talking about something and being able to stay with
      it.</p>
      <p>For therapists: I have supervised LPC Associates since 2009, online anywhere in Texas,
      and I run mindful trail retreats for interns, associates and therapists, where we look at
      being present and at what gets in the way of it.</p>
